  "id": "Compound::DB00508 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Triflupromazine is likely to interact with Chrm1 through a mechanism involving muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. The evidence suggests that Triflupromazine binds to Chrm2, which is involved in the activation of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. This interaction leads to the participation of Chrm2 in the regulation of G-protein coupled neurotransmitter receptor activity, ultimately resulting in the activation of Chrm1. The consistent binding to Chrm2 and the involvement of muscarinic receptors in the activation of Chrm1 indicate a potential mechanism by which Triflupromazine influences Chrm1 function. The overlapping pathways across the different evidence paths highlight a shared role of Chrm2 in mediating Triflupromazine's effects on Chrm1, suggesting a robust and well-defined relationship between the two entities.",
